1. the transported isomorphism $\phi^{\rm Hecke}:K_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)$ sends the class of the normalized self-dual indecomposable projective $Y(\lambda)$ to the lower global basis vector $G(\lambda)$ for every $\lambda\in RP$;
2. the transported identification $\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke}:G_0^{\rm gr}(H^{\Lambda})\to V_A(\Lambda_0)^\vee$ sends $[D(\lambda)]$ to the upper global basis vector $G^*(\lambda)$, form-dual to $G(\lambda)$ under the highest-weight contravariant form;
3. equivalently the lower global basis is the canonical basis of the basic module $V_A(\Lambda_0)$ and corresponds to projective indecomposables, while the upper global basis is the dual-canonical basis and corresponds to simple classes, and the labels are those of [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (4): the crystal $B(\Lambda_0)=RP$, with a simple and its projective cover carrying the same label. The passage from these crystal labels to the cellular/Dipper-James parametrization of the simple modules is not asserted here; it is established with the head classification later on this page;
4. specializing the grading $q=1$ via the forgetful maps $\zeta$ of [[def-graded-module-and-projective-grothendieck-groups-for-klr-towers]] recovers Ariki's ungraded canonical-basis statement, and combined with the graded decomposition theorem later on this page it gives his ungraded decomposition-number theorem.

